.TH GPSTREAM 8 "2026-09-30" "gpstream 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gpstream \- high-throughput logical copy of data between Greenplum 6 clusters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpstream
.I command
.RI " [ flags ]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpstream
copies logical data between two Greenplum 6 clusters over a direct high-speed
network (source -> destination)
.B "without any intermediate storage"
, with snapshot consistency, crash recovery, background index rebuild,
redistribution accounting, and a fully deterministic
.BR --dry-run
plan.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Execute the copy: stream source to dest with no intermediate storage.
.TP
.B dry-run
Plan the copy without executing (\fB--timeout\fR S bounds the plan; the plan JSON is always persisted to \fB<state-dir>/<operation-id>/plan.json\fR).
.TP
.B bench
Measure the input stream: stream the selected source tables through the real
data path (\fBCOPY ... TO STDOUT\fR \-> frame v2 \-> compression) and discard
the payload instead of writing a
destination. It reports the source's production rate \emph{before} the
network/destination hop, so the operator can size \fB--workers\fR and
compare the input rate against \fB--rate-limit\fR (the value does not
throttle the benchmark — the pacer is not wired into the data path yet).
Only the source is used;
\fB--dest\fR (if given) is ignored. Per-table and total rows, wire and
logical bytes, elapsed and MB/s are printed. \fB--timeout\fR S is the
benchmark wall-time limit (unlimited when not passed); all other
selection/performance options (CLI > env > toml) are honored exactly as for
\fBrun\fR.
.TP
.B status
Report operation state (\fB--operation-id\fR id).
.TP
.B resume
Resume an interrupted operation (\fB--operation-id\fR id); re-queues non-terminal units, skips completed/swapped/failed/skipped.
.TP
.B cleanup
Remove operation state (\fB--operation-id\fR id, \fB--yes\fR required).
.B --all-incomplete
removes every operation dir with non-terminal units.
.BR --mode
drop
.BR --dest
<connstring> also drops the destination objects this operation created
(surviving staging, renamed-in or created final tables, renamed-in schemas)
to restore the pre-operation state.
.TP
.B doctor
Self-diagnose source/dest clusters; output PASS/FAIL/WARN.
.TP
.B selftest
Internal sanity checks: schema, checksums, limiter, plan determinism, framing.
.TP
.B version
Print the binary version.
.TP
.B help
Print the command-line help. Also accepted as
.BR --help
or
.BR -h .
.SH OPTIONS (run / dry-run)
.SS Selection
.TP
.BR --source
connstr
Source cluster connection string.
.TP
.BR --dest
connstr
Destination cluster connection string. Segment-to-segment writes
(\fB--copy-mode\fR on_segment: routed, direct_1to1) build per-segment
utility DSNs from it and therefore require the URL form
(postgres://user@host:port/db); a keyword/value form silently degrades
the run to the merge path through the destination coordinator (doctor
reports it as a WARN).
.TP
.BR --dbs
csv
One or more databases (comma-separated).
.TP
.BR --schemas
csv
One or more schemas,
.IR db.schema .
.TP
.BR --tables
csv
One or more tables,
.IR db.schema.table .
.TP
.BR --exclude
csv
Exclude a database/schema/table.
.TP
.BR --metadata-only
Copy the structure only (table DDL and sequences, no data). Idempotent: a
repeated run re-creates nothing that already exists.
.TP
.BR --data-only
Copy data only, into the EXISTING destination structure (no DDL): the
destination rows are replaced by the source rows (TRUNCATE, then load, then
verify). The destination tables must already exist (create them with
.BR --metadata-only
or a full copy first); a missing structure is an error.
.I
--metadata-only
and
.B --data-only
are mutually exclusive; with neither flag both are copied (the default).
.TP
.BR --map-db | --map-schema | --map-table
src=dst
Create the destination object under another name.
.SS Conflicts
.TP
.BR --resolve-conflicts
stop|skip|overwrite|truncate
Conflict policy. Default
.B stop
: the first conflict stops the run (nothing overwritten, nothing skipped).
.B skip
skips conflicting units (logged to conflicts.jsonl);
.B overwrite
= staging + atomic swap (a destination table that is
referenced by foreign keys on other destination tables has those dependent FK
constraints dropped before the swap; the source FK set is re-applied after the
load, F-11). An existing destination is ALWAYS rewritten — identical data is
re-copied, never detected and skipped (a pre-copy content comparison would
read the whole table twice, which is too expensive for large tables; the
only content check is the post-copy
.BR --verify );
.B truncate
= in-place after TRUNCATE.
.TP
.BR --overwrite | --truncate
Shorthand opt-ins that set the matching policy.
.SS Performance
.TP
.BR --compress
none|zlib|zstd[:1-19]
Stream compression (default zstd, level 3).
.TP
.BR --zstd-level
N
zstd level 1-19 (default 3).
.TP
.BR --rate-limit
rate
Baseline for the ETA estimate and the adaptive load diagnostics; accepts
bps/mbit/gbit (default: none). It does \fBnot\fR cap the flow — the data path
is paced by backpressure only; an enforced ceiling (token-bucket pacer in the
agents) is backlog work (see PERFORMANCE.md).
.TP
.BR --workers
N
Workers per host-pair (default 2). In the routed segment-to-segment data
path (on_segment with different segment counts, or unequal distribution
keys) this also sets the sub-stream fan-out per (source segment,
destination segment) pair: the cluster carries S x D x N segment-to-segment
TCP connections, and a table's rows are split across them round-robin
(after the per-row hash places each row on its destination segment).
.TP
.B --no-routed-write
Disable the routed segment-to-segment data path for S!=D topologies:
on_segment then always merges the source slices through the destination
coordinator (SliceMux) instead of routing in the segreceivers. Off by
default (the routed path is used automatically when available).
.TP
.BR --small-table-threshold
size
Tables smaller than this (default 1MiB) copy with compression off (0 disables).
.TP
.BR --index-workers
N
Background index-build workers (default 2 when the index build is on).
.TP
.B --no-indexes
Skip index creation entirely (master switch:
.BR --index-workers
and
.BR --index-priority
are then ignored; an advisory names the ignored flag(s)).
.TP
.BR --index-priority
size|fifo
Index build order; default
.B size
(largest tables first);
.BR fifo
runs jobs in enqueue order.
.TP
.B --no-constraints
Skip the FK/CHECK constraint-restore pass on the destination.
.SS Transport / resilience
The data path is the two-agent transport (ADR-002): the source agent reads
the table and pushes a compressed stream over a real socket to the dest
agent; the controller is control plane only (no data connections).
The agent and segment binaries
.BR ( gpstream-source-agent ,
.BR gpstream-dest-agent ,
.B gpstream-segreceiver )
are NOT selectable by flag: a packaged install always lays all four in the
same directory, so the controller resolves them by convention — first the
directory of the running
.B gpstream
binary, then the
.B PATH .
Override for dev builds or tarball installs (binaries not beside each other)
via the environment
.BR GPSTREAM_SOURCE_AGENT_BINARY /
.BR GPSTREAM_DEST_AGENT_BINARY /
.BR GPSTREAM_SEG_BINARY
or the config file keys
.BR source_agent_binary /
.BR dest_agent_binary /
.BR seg_binary .
For
.B --copy-mode on_segment
the gpstream package must be installed on every SOURCE segment host (the
kernel runs
.B gpstream-segreceiver
there); the package places it at
.BR /usr/bin/gpstream-segreceiver .
.TP
.BR --copy-mode
coordinator|on_segment
Data path (default coordinator). coordinator = one COPY through each
coordinator; on_segment = the source segments stream in parallel (ON SEGMENT
dispatch) and pick per table:
When the planner proves a table is direct_1to1 (same segment count, equal
distribution keys, same GP major), on_segment writes directly into the
destination segments (no merge, no re-distribution) — logged as
direct_1to1_fastpath. When the segment counts differ (or the keys
differ), on_segment uses the routed segment-to-segment path: each source
segment routes its rows to the destination segments by the distribution
key (S x D x --workers connections) and NEITHER cluster coordinator is in
the data path — logged as routed_write.
.TP
.B --seg-listen
HOST:PORT
Base listen address the segreceivers dial, for
.B --copy-mode
on_segment. Optional: when omitted it auto-resolves to the controller's
primary non-loopback IP plus a free port. Each unit binds
.BR base-port + unitID
(units copy in parallel, so the ports must not collide); the segreceivers on
the source segment hosts dial that address, so the host must be routable from
them — on a multi-homed controller set it explicitly.
.TP
.B --data-port-range
LO-HI|LO
Restrict the automatic data/control port allocation to a fixed range for
strict firewalls (the operator opens exactly that range). Default: fully
automatic — every long-lived agent binds
.BR :0
and reports its real address in the control handshake.
A run needs exactly 3 ports in both copy modes (source control + dest data +
dest control — the on_segment segreceivers dial the dest agent's shared data
listener, so the segment count adds no ports). When set, same-host agents
get pre-bound ports from inside the range and remote (SSH-launched) agents
receive
.B --port-range
and bind their auto-picked ports inside it;
.B --dry-run
verifies the range holds at least 3 ports.
.TP
.B --seg-tls-cert
F
Controller serving certificate for the segment-direct hop. Deliberately separate from
.BR --ssl-*
(the PG connection): the segment stream can be encrypted while the PG connections are not.
.TP
.B --seg-tls-key
F
Serving key for
.BR --seg-tls-cert .
.TP
.B --seg-tls-ca
F
Trusted CA bundle for the segment-direct hop; additionally makes the controller require a
client certificate from the segreceivers (mTLS).
.TP
.BR --frame-ack
on|off
Frame-level ACK + retransmit (default on).
.TP
.BR --ack-timeout
15s|30s|adaptive
ACK timeout (default 30s).
.TP
.BR --max-reconnects
N
Reconnect budget (default 20).
.TP
.BR --retransmit-buffer-max
size
In-flight window per stream (default 64MiB).
.SS Integrity / consistency
.TP
.BR --verify
on|off
Content verification after the copy (default
.B off
). With
.B on
, after each unit lands the content checksum is re-derived on the source
(same snapshot as the data COPY) and on the destination, and a mismatch sets
the unit to
.B failed
(dropping the staging table) — no unverified data is ever swapped in. With
.B off
(no verification) the row count from the COPY protocol is the landed-data
check.
.TP
.BR --auto-resume
N
If units did not reach a terminal state after the run, resume and re-run up
to N times automatically. Default 0 = manual: gpstream does not
retry; the operator runs
.BR "gpstream resume --operation-id"
 id
explicitly.
.TP
.BR --on-unit-failure
continue|abort
continue (default) finishes the rest after a failed unit; abort stops the run.
.SS Planning / operation
.TP
.BR --timeout
S
\fBrun\fR/\fBdry-run\fR: bounds plan formation (default 120s).
\fBbench\fR: the benchmark wall-time limit, unlimited when not passed
(a benchmark capped by default would truncate the number it reports).
.TP
.BR --operation-id
id
Operation identity; state lives at
.BR <state-dir>/<operation-id>/ .
\fBrun\fR: any readable name, or omitted — then a timestamp id
(\fBYYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ\fR UTC, e.g. \fB20261001T153000Z\fR; a \fB-2\fR
suffix is appended on a same-second collision) is generated, so the state
dir is a chronological history of runs and the newest operation is the last
entry. \fBstatus\fR/\fBresume\fR/\fBcleanup\fR: a name, or the magic
value \fBlatest\fR, which resolves to the newest operation folder (by
timestamp name, falling back to mtime for operator-chosen names). The
deterministic plan (CR-4) is always persisted to
.BR <state-dir>/<operation-id>/plan.json
alongside the journal. Note: a timestamp default means a re-run of the same
plan is a \emph{new} operation; to resume an earlier one pass its id (or
\fBlatest\fR).
.TP
.BR --state-dir
dir
Operation state directory (default /var/lib/gpstream).
.TP
.BR --metrics-addr
addr
Serve the Prometheus /metrics endpoint during run.
.TP
.BR --config
file
Config file (TOML or YAML).
.SH TLS
.TP
.BR --ssl-cert | --ssl-key | --ssl-ca | --ssl-min-tls
mTLS client cert/key, CA bundle, minimum TLS version (default 1.2).
The pg data plane is encrypted by default (sslmode=verify-full is enforced
unless the connstring sets an explicit sslmode).
.TP
.BR --control-tls-cert | --control-tls-key | --control-tls-ca
TLS for the control channel (the controller's dials to the agents' control
sockets). The agents serve with their own
.B --control-tls-cert / --control-tls-key ;
the controller verifies each agent's control-socket certificate against
.B --control-tls-ca .
When an agent's
.B --control-tls-ca
enables mTLS, the controller presents
.B --control-tls-cert / --control-tls-key
to that agent.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Configuration precedence: CLI > environment > config file > defaults.
Secrets are taken
.B only
from 0600 files or
.B GPSTREAM_*
environment variables,
.B "never from argv".
There is
.B no
GPSTREAM_SOURCE/DEST variable; endpoints come from --source/--dest or the
config file. Recognised variables (all prefixed GPSTREAM_):
.RI RATE_LIMIT ,
.RI WORKERS ,
.RI COMPRESS ,
.RI AUTO_RESUME ,
.RI ON_UNIT_FAILURE ,
.RI FRAME_ACK ,
.RI ACK_TIMEOUT ,
.RI MAX_RECONNECTS ,
.RI RETRANSMIT_BUFFER_MAX ,
.RI SMALL_TABLE_THRESHOLD ,
.RI SSL_CERT ,
.RI SSL_KEY ,
.RI SSL_CA ,
.RI SEG_TLS_CERT ,
.RI SEG_TLS_KEY ,
.RI SEG_TLS_CA .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpstream(8) ,
docs/ (QUICKSTART, CLI_REFERENCE, CONFIGURATION, METRICS, FAILOVER,
OPERATOR_GUIDE, DOCTOR, TROUBLESHOOTING), docs/adr/000, docs/adr/001.
.SH NOTES
The live destination segment-fill balance (max/avg) is always measured after
each swap and reported; a fixed 1.5 gate emits the WARNING line (the former
.B --segment-balance-threshold
knob was removed).

The redistribution accounting (raw segment skew + layout verdict) is
always computed and reported in the
.B --dry-run
output; there is no gate flag — the operator reads the number and decides.

The journal (durable operation state) is ALWAYS
.BR <state-dir>/<operation-id>/journal.jsonl
, mirrored to the destination service DB (gpstream_units). Recovery is
journal-driven:
.BR "gpstream resume"
re-queues non-terminal units;
.BR "gpstream cleanup"
removes operation state.
